%[text] %[text:anchor:T_CFF3DFB5] # Air Track Collisions Lab
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF3B446B] ## Background
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E40971D4] ### Momentum
%[text] The **momentum** of a body is defined as the product of its mass and velocity:
%[text]  $\\vec{p} = m \\vec{v}, \\qquad (1)$
%[text] where $m$ is the body's mass and $\\vec{v} = \[v\_x, v\_y, v\_z\]$ its velocity vector. The momentum vector, $\\vec{p}$, points in the direction of the velocity vector. The magnitude of the vector is proportional to the mass, as illustrated below.
%[text]  ![momentum.png](text:image:4cb3)
%[text] A body's momentum can be thought of as its impulse: how much of an impact it will have when colliding with another body. Oddly, momentum is not a direct measure of the motion energy of the body.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0FF453F9] ### Kinetic and potential energy
%[text] The **kinetic energy**, $E\_k$, is the energy a body has due to motion. It is defined as
%[text]  $E\_k = \\frac{1}{2} m v^2 \\qquad (2)$
%[text] where $v$ is the magnitude of the velocity vector: $v = \\sqrt{ v\_x^2 + v\_y^2 + v\_z^2 }$. This magnitude $v$ is commonly referred to as the body's **speed**. Note that the kinetic energy has units of energy. For example, a 1 kg mass moving at 1 m/s has 1 Joule of kinetic energy: $E\_k = 1 \\; \\mathrm{kg}\\cdot 1 \\; \\mathrm{m^2/s^2} = 1 \\; \\mathrm{J}$.
%[text] The **gravitational potential energy** of a body is the stored energy a body has due to its position in a gravitational field. The potential energy of a mass near the earth's surface is
%[text] $U = m g h$
%[text] where $g = 9.81 \\; \\mathrm{m/s^2}$ is the acceleration due to gravity near the surface of the earth and $h$ is the height above a reference surface. 
%[text]  ![gravitationalPotential.png](text:image:6b54)
%[text] If the mass is dropped a distance $\\Delta h$, it will gain kinetic energy equal to the amount of potential energy lost (assuming an ideal system without external forces).
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D71E35DD] ### Collisions
%[text] In an isolated system (i.e., in the absence of external forces), the total momentum of the system does not change. This is known as the **conservation of momentum**. For example, consider an isolated system of two masses: $m\_1$ and $m\_2$. If the masses then collide, their total momentum after impact will be equal to their total momentum before impact:
%[text]  $m\_1 \\vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \\vec{v}\_{2,i} =  m\_1 \\vec{v}\_{1,f} +  m\_2 \\vec{v}\_{2,f} \\qquad (3)$
%[text] where $\\vec{v}\_{n,i}$ denotes the velocity of the $n^{th$ mass before collision and $\\vec{v}\_{n,f}$ after collision.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E745ADE2] #### Elastic collisions
%[text] When the two masses collide momentum will always be conserved. However, the kinetic energy will not always be conserved. Some of it may be transferred into another form of energy. In some collisions, known as **elastic collisions**, kinetic energy **is** conserved (along with momentum). For a two-mass system:
%[text]  $\\frac{1}{2}m\_1 v\_{1,i}^2 +  \\frac{1}{2}m\_2 v\_{2,i}^2 =  \\frac{1}{2}m\_1 v\_{1,f}^2 +  \\frac{1}{2}m\_2 v\_{2,f}^2 \\qquad (4)$
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![elastic\_collision2.png](text:image:01e8)
%[text]{"align":"center"} **Figure 1**. An elastic collision.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_62C5C53F] #### Inelastic collisions
%[text] In an inelastic collision, kinetic energy is not conserved (although the momentum is still conserved). For example, consider a collision where the two bodies become rigidly attached during the collision.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![inelastic\_collision.png](text:image:0ede)
%[text]{"align":"center"} **Figure 2**. An inelastic collision.
%[text] In this case, the velocity of the two bodies is the same after the collision: $\\vec{v}\_{1,f} = \\vec{v}\_{2,f} = \\vec{v}\_f$. The momentum conservation equation (3) becomes:
%[text] $m\_1 \\vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \\vec{v}\_{2,i} =  (m\_1 + m\_2) \\vec{v}\_{f}$.
%[text] This equation has only one solution for $\\vec{v}\_f$: 
%[text] $\\vec{v}\_{f} = \\frac{m\_1 \\vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \\vec{v}\_{2,i} }{m\_1 + m\_2}$.
%[text] This solution is incompatible with the conservation of energy in almost all cases.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_35ED2D5B] ## Experiment overview
%[text] In this experiment, you will use an air track to investigate collisions and energy exchange in 1D. The air track reduces friction in the system by providing a cushion of air below the carts. This isolates the system (to a point), so that conservation of momentum may be observed. The virtual air track has been designed to have similar properties to a physical air track.
%[text] Three variations of the air track experimental setup will be used to analyze various exchanges of energy and momentum:
%[text] 1. An elastic bumper on the carts. In a physical air track, a rubber band is used on one cart, with a flag on the other. In this case, the mechanical energy is largely conserved. You will use this variation to observe a (nearly) perfect elastic collision.
%[text] 2. A wax tube and needle on the carts. In this case, the carts become fused after colliding. You will use this variation to observe a (nearly) perfectly inelastic collision.
%[text] 3. A pulley and hanging mass connected to a single cart. This setup is used to accelerate the cart using the potential energy of the hanging mass. You will use this variation to observe the transfer of mechanical energy and estimate the acceleration due to gravity. \
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![cartDiagram.png](text:image:8916)
%[text]{"align":"center"} **Figure 3.** Air track experimental setup.
%[text] **Air track details**
%[text] - Each cart weighs 180g and is 15cm long.
%[text] - You can add additional weight to the carts using the model sliders.
%[text] - The photogates record the time it takes for the full length of the cart to pass through.
%[text] - The air track is 2m long.  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4114F2CE] ## Pre-lab Questions
%[text] **Question 1.** Suppose a cart passes through a photogate in 0.6 seconds. What was the speed of the cart in m/s?
v_q1 = 0.25;    % Write the speed in m/s
%[text] **Question 2.** Suppose that the carts have no additional weights and have been equipped with rubber bands and flags (resulting in elastic collisions). If cart 1 begins at rest and cart 2 approaches from the $+x$ direction at -0.3 m/s, what velocities will the carts have after collision?
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![prelabq1.png](text:image:0bf7)
%[text]  Since the carts can only move in 1D, your answer only requires the $x$-component: $v\_x$.
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] Conservation of momentum:
%[text] $v\_{1f} + v\_{2f} = -0.3$
%[text] Conservation of energy:
%[text] $0.09  = v\_{1f}^2 + v\_{2f}^2$
%[text] Solving the system:
%[text] $v\_{1f} = - 0.3 \\text{ m/s}, \\ \\ v\_{2f} = 0 \\text{ m/s}$
v1_q2 = -0.3    % Write the final velocity of cart 1 
v2_q2 = 0       % Write the final velocity of cart 2 
%[text] **Hint:** Use conservation of momentum (3) and conservation of kinetic energy (4).
%[text] **Question 3.** Repeat question 2 but suppose that two additional 100 g weights were added to cart 2. 
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] Conservation of momentum:
%[text] $180v\_{1f} + 380v\_{2f} = -114$
%[text] Conservation of energy:
%[text] $34.2  = 180v\_{1f}^2 + 380v\_{2f}^2$
%[text] Solving the system:
%[text] $v\_{1f} = -\\frac{57}{140} \\text{ m/s}, \\ \\ v\_{2f} = -\\frac{3}{28} \\text{ m/s}$
v1_q3 = -57/140 % Write the final velocity of cart 1 
v2_q3 = -3/28   % Write the final velocity of cart 2 
%[text] **Question 4.** Suppose that the rubber bands have been replaced with wax/needle pairs (resulting in the carts sticking together after collision). Cart 1 has a single extra 100 g mass, while cart 2 has no extra masses. Suppose the carts are accelerated toward each other with cart 1 having a velocity of 0.4 m/s and cart 2 having a velocity of -0.5 m/s, what will their velocities be after collision?
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] $v\_{f} = \\frac{m\_1 v\_{1,i} +  m\_2 v\_{2,i} }{m\_1 + m\_2} = \\frac{ 280(0.4) + 180(-0.5) }{280 + 180} = \\frac{11}{230} \\text{ m/s}$
v1_q4 = 11/230  % Write the final velocity of cart 1 
v2_q4 = 11/230  % Write the final velocity of cart 2 
%[text] **Hint:** The two carts will have the same velocity after collision, since they are fused together.
%[text] **Question 5.** Suppose a mass weighing 150 g is dropped from a height of 2 meters above the ground. What will its speed be at a height of 0.5 m above the ground?
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] $mgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$
%[text] $(9.81)(1.5) = \\frac{1}{2}v^2$
%[text] $v = 5.42 \\text{ m/s}$
v_q5 = sqrt(29.43)  % Write the speed in m/s
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_84CD4E24] ## Virtual Experiment
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8AA5F0BC] ### **Explore the virtual experimental setup**
%[text] 1. Open the first Simscape Multibody model: [airTrackElastic.slx](<matlab:  airTrackElastic>) (to open the model using this link, it must be on your [MATLAB Search Path](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_env/what-is-the-matlab-search-path.html)).
%[text] 2. Run the model ![clock.png](text:image:75cc) and observe it in the Mechanics Explorer.
%[text] 3. Use the controls to push the carts. You should push the carts while the model is still running.
%[text] 4. When you push a cart and it passes through the photogate, the time for the cart to pass through is recorded in the readout. Try pushing the cart harder by holding the `push` button longer.
%[text] 5. Stop ![stop\_16 (1).png](text:image:475b) the simulation to reset it.
%[text] 6. Adjust the starting positions by double-clicking the air track model labeled: "Double click here to adjust the starting positions and weights."
%[text] 7. Review the simulation by adjusting the slider at the bottom of the Mechanics Explorer window: ![slider.png](text:image:2326) \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:9f31)
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:5d39)
%[text]{"align":"center"} **Figure 4.** How to use the Simscape Multibody air track model
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1A2CA67B] ### Part 1
%[text] 1. Ensure that the carts have no added mass.
%[text] 2. Give cart 1 a push in $+x$ direction. Record the time required for cart 1 to pass through gate 1.
%[text] 3. Record the time required for cart 2 to pass through gate 2. Stop the model quickly after the collision or the stop clock memory will be overwritten. Write your answer in the space provided below.
%[text] 4. Estimate the velocities of the carts before and after the collision and write your answers below. \
% Times for carts to pass through gates
t0_cart1_p1 = 0.2078;
t0_cart2_p1 = 0.2179;
% Velocities before collision (in m/s)
v0_p1_cart1 = 0.15/0.2078
v0_p1_cart2 = 0
% Velocities after collision (in m/s)
vf_p1_cart1 = 0
vf_p1_cart2 = 0.15/0.2179
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_966D4665] ### Part 2
%[text] 1. Add 200 g to cart 2. To do this, double click on the air track model and adjust the slider for "mass added to second cart."
%[text] 2. Repeat the experiment from Part 1. Note that this time you should also record the time for cart 1 to pass back through gate 1 after collision. \
% Time required for cart 2 to pass through the gate as it approaches cart1:
t0_p2_cart1 = 0.1085;
tf_p2_cart1 = 0.2971;
tf_p2_cart2 = 0.1622;
% Velocities before collision (in m/s)
v0_p2_cart1 = 0.15/0.1085
v0_p2_cart2 = 0
% Velocities after collision (in m/s)
vf_p2_cart1 = -0.15/0.2971
vf_p2_cart2 = 0.15/0.1622
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F4554C83] ### Part 3
%[text] 1. Close the first model and open the second Simscape Multibody model: [airTrackInelastic.slx](<matlab:  airTrackInelastic>).
%[text] 2. Add 300 g to cart 1 and remove any additional weight from cart 2.
%[text] 3. Push cart 1 in the $+x$ direction.
%[text] 4. Record the photogate times. Note that when the two carts pass through the gate together, their times are still recorded separately by the photogate. You can choose the cart time from one of the carts. \
% Times for carts to pass through gates
t0_p3_cart1 = 0.2534;
t0_p3_carts = 0.366;
% Velocities before collision (in m/s)
v0_p3_cart1 = 0.15/0.2534
v0_p3_cart2 = 0
% Velocity after collision (in m/s)
vf_p3_carts = 0.15/0.366
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0AE8295A] ### Part 4
%[text] 1. Close the second model and open the final Simscape Multibody model: [airTrackHangingMass.slx](<matlab:  airTrackHangingMass>).
%[text] 2. Set the hanging mass to 50 g by double-clicking the "Air Track Simscape Model" and add 200 g to cart 2.
%[text] 3. Place cart 2 at $x = 0$, gate 1 at $x = 0.3$, and gate 2 at $x = 0.6$.
%[text] 4. Run the model without any pushes. Record the time required for the cart to pass through each gate the first time the mass falls.
%[text] 5. Compute the velocity at each position and record them below. \
% Stop clock times
t1_p4 = 0.2025;
t2_p4 = 0.1370;
% Velocities at x=0.3 and x=0.6
v1_p4 = 0.15/0.2025
v2_p4 = 0.15/0.1370
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_00BDB8AB] ## Data Processing & Analysis 
%[text] In this section, you will explore the concepts of conservation of momentum and energy using the data collected above. In Parts 1-3 of the experiment, you recorded the velocities of the carts before and after a collision. You can use these measurements to compute the momentum and kinetic energy of each cart. If a quantity is conserved, it should be the same before and after the collision. To determine how close this system is to ideal, compute the following for Parts 1-3:
%[text] 1. the total momentum before and after collision
%[text] 2. the total energy before and after collision
%[text] 3. the percentage of momentum lost
%[text] 4. the percentage of energy lost \
%[text] **Note:**
%[text] - To compute the % loss use: $\\% \\text{loss} = \\frac{p\_0-p\_f}{p\_0} \\times 100\\%$
%[text] - Each cart has a mass of 180 g = 0.18 kg (+ any added mass).
%[text] - Give masses in terms of kilograms so that the momenta are given in kg$\\cdot$m/s and energy is given in Joules (1 J = 1 kg$\\cdot$m$^2$/s$^2$). \
%%
%[text] **Part 1**
%[text] In Part 1 of the experiment, you used a model with elastic collision dynamics. Do you expect momentum and/or energy to be conserved in this case?
%[text] **Task.** Compute the quantities described using your measurements from Part 1 of the experiment and write your answers below.
%[text] **Note**: replace **NaN** with the answer in each case. 
m1 = 180*10^-3;
m2 = 180*10^-3; 
v01 = 0.15/0.2078;
v02 = 0;
vf1 = 0;
vf2 = 0.688;

% total momentum before/after collision (kg*m/s)
p0_da1 = m1*v01 + m2*v02
pf_da1 = m1*vf1 + m2*vf2
% total energy before/after collision (J)
ek0_da1 = 0.5*m1*(v01)^2
ekf_da1 =  0.5*m1*(vf1)^2 + 0.5*m2*(vf2)^2
% percentage of momentum lost
pLost_da1 = (p0_da1 - pf_da1)/p0_da1*100
% percentage of energy lost
ekLost_da1 = (ek0_da1 - ekf_da1)/ek0_da1*100
%%
%[text] **Part 2**
%[text] In Part 2 of the experiment, you used the same model with elastic collision dynamics.
%[text] **Task.** Compute the quantities described using your measurements from Part 2 of the experiment and write your answers below.
m1 = 180*10^-3;
m2 = (180+200)*10^-3;
v01 = 1.38;
v02 = 0;
vf1 = -0.5049;
vf2 = 0.9248;

% total momentum before/after collision (kg*m/s)
p0_da2 = m1*v01 + m2*v02
pf_da2 = m1*vf1 + m2*vf2
% total energy before/after collision (J)
ek0_da2 = 0.5*m1*v01^2 + 0.5*m2*v02^2
ekf_da2 =  0.5*m1*vf1^2 + 0.5*m2*vf2^2

% percentage of momentum lost
pLost_da2 = (p0_da2 - pf_da2)/p0_da2*100
% percentage of energy lost
ekLost_da2 = (ek0_da2 - ekf_da2)/ek0_da2*100
%%
%[text] **Part 3**
%[text] In Part 3 of the experiment, the carts become fused after collision. This characterizes an inelastic collision.
%[text] **Task.** Compute the quantities described using your measurements from Part 3 of the experiment and write your answers below.
m1 = (180+300)*10^-3;
m2 = (180)*10^-3;
v01 = 0.5919;
v02 = 0;
vf1 = 0.4098;

% total momentum before/after collision (kg*m/s)
p0_da3 = m1*v01 + m2*v02
pf_da3 = (m1+m2)*vf1 
% total energy before/after collision (J)
ek0_da3 = 0.5*m1*v01^2 + 0.5*m2*v02^2
ekf_da3 =  0.5*(m1+m2)*vf1^2 
% percentage of momentum lost
pLost_da3 = (p0_da3 - pf_da3)/p0_da3*100
% percentage of energy lost
ekLost_da3 = (ek0_da3 - ekf_da3)/ek0_da3*100
%[text] **Question.** In cases where the momentum should be conserved, momentum is lost. What is responsible for the momentum loss?
q_da3 = "Friction"; % Type your answer in the quotes
%%
%[text] In Part 4 of the experiment, energy is converted from potential to kinetic energy (and vice versa). You can identify how much of the energy is kinetic and potential in each state using the experimental measurements.
%[text] **Part 4**
%[text] For the initial state and the two recorded states (when the cart passes through gate 1 and when the cart passes through gate 2), compute:
%[text] 1. potential energy
%[text] 2. kinetic energy
%[text] 3. total energy \
mc = (180+200)*10^-3; % Cart mass
mh = 50*10^-3; % Hanging mass
v0 = 0;
v1 = 0.15/0.1912;
v2 = 0.15/0.1323;

% initial state
ep0_da4 = mh*9.81*0.9       % potential energy (J)
ek0_da4 = 0                 % kinetic energy (J)
e0_da4 = ep0_da4 + ek0_da4  % total energy (J)

% state 1: passing through gate 1
ep1_da4 = mh*9.81*0.6       % potential energy (J)
ek1_da4 = 0.5*(mc+mh)*v1^2  % kinetic energy (J)
e1_da4 = ep1_da4 + ek1_da4  % total energy (J)

% state 2: passing through gate 2
ep2_da4 = mh*9.81*0.3       % potential energy (J)
ek2_da4 = 0.5*(mc+mh)*v2^2  % kinetic energy (J)
e2_da4 = ep2_da4 + ek2_da4  % total energy (J)
%[text] **Question.** Should the total energy be conserved in this system? Do the observations match what you expect?
q_da4 = "Yes. Yes, the observations show that the energy is mostly conserved. The losses are consistent with frictional losses."; % Type your answer in the quotes
%%
